.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt FUJICLI-FML 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fujicli-fml
.Nd Fuji Modelling Language
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The Fuji Modelling Language (FML) describes the camera options, cameras,
processors, and sensors that
.Xr fujicli 1
supports, and the rules that relate option values to each other.
It is a CUE schema in the
.Pa fml/
directory of the source tree, package
.Ql fml .
.Pp
At build time,
.Ql cue export ./fml --out json
turns the package into a single JSON document, which is compiled into
.Xr fujicli 1 .
Editing FML and rebuilding is how support for a camera or option is added.
.Pp
The document has five top-level keys:
.Cm profile ,
the conversion profile layout, and the maps
.Cm options ,
.Cm cameras ,
.Cm processors ,
and
.Cm sensors .
Each map entry has an
.Cm id ,
which is set to its map key and need not be written, and a
.Cm spec .
Ids are
.Ql snake_case
and are used as Rust identifiers and command-line flag names.
Entries reference each other by id.
.Pp
CUE checks types, references, and the constraints below when the package is
exported.
The code generator rejects any key it does not know.
Keys whose names begin with an underscore are hidden: they can be used within
CUE but are not exported and the code generator never sees them.
.Sh OPTIONS
An option is one camera setting with a typed value.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
options: <id>: #Option & {
    spec:     {...}
    codegen?: {...}
}
.Ed
.Ss spec
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm name : Ar string
Human-readable name.
Required.
.It Cm category : Ar string
Group under which the option's flag is listed in
.Xr fujicli 1
help output.
Optional.
.It Cm kind : Ar kind
The value type, one of
.Cm integer ,
.Cm float ,
.Cm string ,
or
.Cm enum .
Required.
.It Cm rules : Ar object
Constraints on the value; see
.Sx Value rules .
Required for
.Cm enum ,
optional otherwise.
.It Cm encoding : Ar object
How the value is represented on the wire; see
.Sx Encodings .
Required.
.It Cm default : Ar value
The value of the generated type's
.Ql Default .
Optional; see
.Sx Defaults .
.El
.Ss Value rules
For
.Cm integer
and
.Cm float :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm min : Ar number
Smallest value.
.It Cm max : Ar number
Largest value.
At least
.Cm min .
.It Cm step : Ar number
Values must be multiples of
.Cm step .
Greater than 0, and a positive integer for
.Cm integer .
Default 1.
.El
.Pp
For
.Cm string :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm min_length : Ar uint
Shortest length.
.It Cm max_length : Ar uint
Longest length.
At least
.Cm min_length .
.El
.Pp
For
.Cm enum :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm variants : Ar list
The values, in order.
At least one.
Each variant has:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm id : Ar string
Identifier used in predicates and lookup tables.
Unique within the option.
.It Cm name : Ar string
Display name.
.It Cm aliases : Ar list
Further names accepted on the command line for this variant.
Optional.
.El
.Pp
On the command line a variant is written as its
.Cm id
or one of its aliases, with underscores replaced by hyphens, for example
.Ql classic-chrome
or
.Ql mono-yellow ,
and matched exactly except for case.
These names must be unique across the option's variants, ignoring case.
Exported simulations refer to variants by
.Cm id ,
so renaming an id breaks files exported before the change.
.El
.Ss Encodings
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind : Qq Cm raw
The wire value is the value itself.
The only encoding allowed for
.Cm string .
.It Cm kind : Qq Cm scale
The wire value is the value multiplied by
.Cm spec.scale ,
a positive integer.
For
.Cm integer
and
.Cm float .
.It Cm kind : Qq Cm lookup
The wire value is looked up in
.Cm spec.values ,
a non-empty map from value to wire value.
The only encoding allowed for
.Cm enum ,
whose map must have exactly one key per variant id.
For
.Cm integer
and
.Cm float ,
each key is the value written as a string, for example
.Qq -1
or
.Qq 0.7 ,
and must lie within
.Cm min
and
.Cm max
and be a multiple of
.Cm step .
A map value is an integer, or a list of integers whose first element is written
and whose other elements are also accepted when reading.
.El
.Pp
Every encoding may set
.Cm prop_code ,
a 16-bit PTP device property code.
An option with a property code can be read and written on its own, which
simulation settings require.
An option without one exists only as a render profile field.
.Pp
Wire values are 16 bits wide.
Code generation fails if an option's range or lookup values fit neither a
signed nor an unsigned 16-bit integer, so
.Cm raw
and
.Cm scale
options need both
.Cm min
and
.Cm max .
.Ss Defaults
If set,
.Cm default
must satisfy the option's rules: be within
.Cm min
and
.Cm max ,
be a multiple of
.Cm step ,
be one of the lookup keys for a numeric
.Cm lookup
option, be a variant id for an
.Cm enum ,
and respect
.Cm min_length
and
.Cm max_length .
A
.Cm string
option with a positive
.Cm min_length
must set it.
.Pp
Otherwise the default is the first variant of an
.Cm enum ,
the key of smallest magnitude of a numeric
.Cm lookup ,
the multiple of
.Cm step
within range nearest zero for
.Cm raw
and
.Cm scale ,
and the empty string for a
.Cm string .
.Ss codegen
Optional.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm skip : Cm true
Do not offer the option as a command-line flag.
Its type is still generated, for use by
.Xr fujicli 1
itself.
.It Cm flaky : Cm true
When writing a simulation, if the camera rejects this setting, log a warning
and continue with the remaining settings instead of failing.
.El
.Sh CAMERAS\&
A camera is one supported model.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cameras: <id>: #Camera & {
    spec: {...}
}
.Ed
.Ss spec
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm name : Ar string
The model name.
Required.
.It Cm sensor : Ar id
The camera's image sensor.
Required.
.It Cm processor : Ar id
The camera's image processor.
Required.
.It Cm usb : Ar object
Required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm vendor_id : Ar uint16
USB vendor ID.
Default
.Cm 0x04cb .
.It Cm product_id : Ar uint16
USB product ID.
Required.
.It Cm chunk_size : Ar uint
Maximum size in bytes of each transfer.
Greater than 0.
Default
.Cm 1048576 .
.El
.It Cm features : Ar object
What the camera supports.
A missing feature is unsupported.
Optional.
.El
.Ss features
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm backup : Cm true
The camera supports backing up and restoring its settings.
.It Cm simulation : Ar object
The camera stores film simulations in custom setting slots.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm slots : Ar uint
Number of custom setting slots, starting at C1.
Required.
.It Cm settings : Ar list
The settings that make up a simulation, in order.
Each is
.Bro Cm id , ref Brc ,
where
.Cm ref
names an option and
.Cm id ,
which defaults to
.Cm ref ,
names the setting in predicates.
Ids are unique.
Required.
.It Cm rules : Ar list
See
.Sx RULES .
.El
.It Cm render : Ar object
The camera converts RAF files using a conversion profile.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm fields : Ar list
The ids of the profile fields the camera uses; see
.Sx PROFILE .
Required.
.It Cm requests : Ar list
See
.Sx REQUESTS .
.It Cm transformations : Ar list
See
.Sx TRANSFORMATIONS .
.It Cm rules : Ar list
See
.Sx RULES .
.El
.El
.Pp
Rules, requests, and transformations refer to settings and fields by id, and
may only refer to those of their own block.
Rules and requests may only refer to settings.
.Sh PROCESSORS AND SENSORS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
processors: <id>: #Processor & {
    spec: name: string
}

sensors: <id>: #Sensor & {
    spec: name: string
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm name
is the display name, and is the only exported key.
Codegen uses a camera's processor to order the list of supported cameras.
.Sh TRAITS
A trait is a template for the cameras that match it, in the hidden list
.Cm _traits .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
_traits: [...#Trait]

#Trait: {
    when: {sensor?: id, processor?: id}
    simulation: {settings: [...], rules: [...]}
    render: {fields: [...], requests: [...], transformations: [...], rules: [...]}
}
.Ed
.Pp
A trait matches a camera when every key in its
.Cm when
equals the camera's, so it can target a processor, a sensor, or one
combination of both.
Each list defaults to empty.
.Pp
Within a camera's
.Cm spec ,
.Cm _template
holds the lists of every matching trait, concatenated in
.Cm _traits
order; a camera declares
.Ql _template: _
in its own
.Cm spec
to refer to it.
Templates are copied explicitly, and can be extended with
.Ql list.Concat .
Codegen sees only the result.
.Sh PROFILE
The conversion profile is a list of 32-bit fields, in wire order, shared by
every camera.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
profile: fields: [...#Field]
.Ed
.Pp
A field with a
.Cm ref
is a setting, and takes its type and encoding from that option.
Its
.Cm id
defaults to
.Cm ref .
A field without one is an inline integer with no encoding, and must have an
.Cm id .
Ids are unique.
.Pp
A camera's render uses the fields it lists.
A setting without a value is written as 0.
Fields a camera does not list are written back as the camera reported them,
and ignored when read.
A camera may send back fewer fields than it is sent.
.Sh PREDICATES
A predicate is a test on a block's values.
It is a leaf, a combination of predicates, or a boolean literal.
.Ss Leaves
A leaf has
.Cm ref ,
the id of a setting, an optional
.Cm scope ,
and exactly one test:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm present : Ar bool
The field has a value, or with
.Cm false ,
has none.
.It Cm equals : Ar value
The field equals the value.
.It Cm in : Ar list
The field equals one of the values.
.It Cm min : Ar number , Cm max : Ar number
The field is between the bounds, inclusive.
.It Cm lt , lte , gt , gte : Ar number
The field is less than, at most, greater than, or at least the value.
.El
.Pp
Every test but
.Cm present
is unknown when the field has no value, and so is its negation.
A predicate holds only when it is true; to match a field without a value, test
.Cm present : false .
.Cm present ,
.Cm equals ,
and
.Cm in
apply to any field; the others only to
.Cm integer
and
.Cm float .
Values must have the field's type, and
.Cm enum
values must be variant ids.
Float equality allows for rounding error.
.Ss Combinations
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm all : Ar list
All of the predicates hold.
An empty list is true.
False if any is false, otherwise unknown if any is unknown.
.It Cm any : Ar list
At least one of the predicates holds.
An empty list is false.
True if any holds, otherwise unknown if any is unknown.
.It Cm not : Ar predicate
The predicate does not hold.
Unknown if the predicate is unknown.
.It Cm true , false
Always and never.
.El
.Ss Scope
.Cm scope
selects which values a leaf tests:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm current
The values being validated or written.
The default.
.It Cm original
The render profile as read from the image, with transformations undone,
before the user's changes.
Only allowed in render rules.
Original values are never changed: they are ignored when deciding how to
repair a rule or whether to read a field.
.El
.Sh RULES
A rule names a combination of values that is invalid or noteworthy.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm severity : Ar severity
.Cm error ,
.Cm warning ,
or
.Cm info .
Default
.Cm error .
.It Cm message : Ar string
Shown when the rule fires.
Required.
.It Cm when : Ar predicate
The rule fires when this holds.
Required.
.El
.Pp
Rules test settings, not the values transformations write.
.Ss Errors
Values may not be written while an error rule fires.
When the user changes settings, the new values are merged into the current
ones, requests are applied, and each error rule that fires is then repaired,
in order.
.Pp
Repair rewrites
.Cm when
as an
.Cm any
of conjunctions, each an
.Cm all
of leaves, and changes one field in each conjunction that holds so that it no
longer does.
If the field is optional, it first tries clearing it.
For a negated
.Cm equals ,
.Cm in ,
or
.Cm min Ns / Ns Cm max ,
it then tries setting the field to the value named, the first value listed, or
.Cm min .
It never changes an original value, or a setting the user set.
A setting given a
.Cm keep
request counts as not set.
A change is undone if it makes a rule that was satisfied fire, or leaves the
rule being repaired firing.
If no change works, the update fails with the rule's
.Cm message .
Each change is logged as a warning.
.Pp
Importing a simulation file sets every setting in it, so a file that breaks a
rule is refused.
Reading a simulation or a render profile does not repair it.
.Pp
Order matters.
Rules are repaired one at a time, and repairing one rule can leave a later rule
without a repair.
.Ss Read gating
An error rule conjunction of the form
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{all: [{ref: "x", present: true}, C...]}
.Ed
.Pp
says that
.Ql x
has no meaning while the conditions
.Ar C
hold.
It also gates reading:
.Ql x
is read from the camera, or from a render profile, only if not all of
.Ar C
hold, and the fields that
.Ar C
test are read first.
If several conjunctions gate
.Ql x ,
all their gates must allow the read.
A conjunction that instead requires
.Ql x
absent, with
.Cm present : false
and no
.Cm present : true
leaf, gates
.Ql x
the other way: it is read only if all of
.Ar C
hold.
Original-scope leaves do not gate.
.Pp
Gated fields are optional in the camera's generated simulation or render
profile type.
All other fields are required: they are always read and must have a value.
.Pp
A test on
.Ql x
requires a value, so a conjunction with both it and
.Cm present : true
validates
.Ql x
without gating it, and one with
.Cm present : false
never holds.
Codegen fails if gates form a cycle.
Apart from gating, fields are processed in declaration order.
.Ss Warnings and information
After a successful repair, each
.Cm warning
or
.Cm info
rule that holds is logged at that level with its
.Cm message .
Such rules never block, repair, or gate.
.Sh REQUESTS
A request marks an option value that a render cannot have, and says what it
means.
Each is
.Bro Cm ref , value Brc
on an
.Cm enum
setting, with one of:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm keep : Cm true
The value, given by the user, counts as not given, so the image's value is
kept.
.It Cm becomes : Ar variant
The value, given by the user or as shot, is replaced by this variant.
.El
.Pp
Requests only apply to renders.
Codegen fails if a value has two requests, if a
.Cm becomes
variant has a request of its own, or if repair could set a setting to a value
that has a request; see
.Sx Errors .
.Sh TRANSFORMATIONS
A render transformation writes raw values to profile fields when a predicate
on the settings holds.
Transformations encode setting values that the camera stores differently.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm when : Ar predicate
The condition.
Optional; without it the transformation always applies.
Current scope only.
.It Cm apply : Ar list
Writes, each
.Bro Cm ref , value Brc ,
where
.Cm ref
is a listed field, and
.Cm value
is an integer or, for an
.Cm enum
setting, a variant, which is written as its encoded value.
Required.
.El
.Pp
When a render profile is written, inline fields start at 0, and each
transformation whose
.Cm when
holds then overwrites the fields it writes, in order.
.Pp
A transformation whose
.Cm when
is an
.Cm equals ,
or an
.Cm all
of
.Cm equals
on different settings, encodes those values.
It can be undone if it writes a value that nothing else puts in that field:
a value other than 0 in an inline field, or a value other than 0 and the
encoded values in a lookup-encoded setting, that no other transformation
writes there.
When a render profile is read and every field such a transformation writes
holds its value, the settings it encodes take their values from its
.Cm when
and are not read.
Reading fails if that value is there without the rest, or if a setting's gate
excludes it.
.Pp
Codegen fails unless:
.Bl -bullet
.It
every listed inline field is written by some transformation;
.It
only a transformation that encodes values writes a setting, and only one of
the settings it encodes;
.It
a transformation that writes a setting can be undone;
.It
a later transformation that writes a field of one that can be undone cannot
hold where that one does;
.It
two transformations that can be undone, and encode different values for a
setting, write different values to a field they both write.
.El
.Sh COMMAND LINE
Each option without
.Cm codegen.skip
becomes a flag named after its id, with underscores replaced by hyphens, for
example
.Fl -film-simulation
for
.Ql film_simulation .
Its
.Cm category
is the flag's heading in help output.
Simulation commands take a flag for every such option used as a simulation
setting, and render commands one for every such option used as a render field.
A flag's value is parsed as the option's type: a variant name or alias for an
.Cm enum ,
a number or string otherwise, and checked against the option's rules.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa fml/option.cue
.Ql #Option
and all options.
.It Pa fml/camera.cue
.Ql #Camera
and all cameras.
.It Pa fml/processor.cue
.Ql #Processor
and all processors.
.It Pa fml/sensor.cue
.Ql #Sensor
and all sensors.
.It Pa fml/trait.cue
.Ql #Trait
and all traits.
.It Pa fml/profile.cue
.Ql #Profile
and the profile layout.
.It Pa fml/grammar.cue
Predicates and rules.
.It Pa fml/main.cue
The definition root.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Validate the schema and inspect the exported document:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cue vet ./fml
$ cue export ./fml --out json | jq '.options.highlight_tone'
.Ed
.Pp
A float option, stored in tenths:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
highlight_tone: #Option & {
    spec: {
        name:     "Highlight Tone"
        category: "Tone"
        kind:     "float"
        rules: {min: -2.0, max: 4.0, step: 0.5}
        encoding: {
            prop_code: 0xD19D
            kind:      "scale"
            spec: scale: 10
        }
    }
}
.Ed
.Pp
An enum option that the camera may refuse to set:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lens_modulation_optimizer: #Option & {
    codegen: flaky: true
    spec: {
        name:     "Lens Modulation Optimizer"
        category: "Lens"
        kind:     "enum"
        rules: variants: [
            {id: "on", name: "On", aliases: ["true"]},
            {id: "off", name: "Off", aliases: ["false"]},
        ]
        encoding: {
            prop_code: 0xD1A3
            kind:      "lookup"
            spec: values: {on: 0x01, off: 0x02}
        }
    }
}
.Ed
.Pp
A camera that takes its simulation from its traits:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
x_s20: #Camera & {
    spec: {
        name:      "FUJIFILM X-S20"
        sensor:    "x_trans_cmos_4"
        processor: "x_processor_5"
        _template: _

        usb: product_id: 0x02f7

        features: {
            backup: true
            simulation: {
                slots:    4
                settings: _template.simulation.settings
                rules:    _template.simulation.rules
            }
        }
    }
}
.Ed
.Pp
A rule against a white balance temperature outside Temperature mode.
To repair it, fujicli clears the temperature or, if the user set the
temperature, selects Temperature mode.
The temperature is read only in Temperature mode.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    message: "White balance temperature is only meaningful in Temperature mode."
    when: all: [
        {ref: "white_balance_temperature", present: true},
        {not: {ref: "white_balance", equals: "temperature"}},
    ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
A rule pinning an option to the values one camera accepts:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    message: "Dynamic Range value is not supported by this camera."
    when: not: {ref: "dynamic_range", in: ["auto", "hdr100", "hdr200", "hdr400"]}
}
.Ed
.Pp
Render requests for Auto Dynamic Range, which keeps the image's value, and for
Fine+RAW, which renders the JPEG part:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{ref: "dynamic_range", value: "auto", keep: true}
{ref: "image_quality", value: "fine_raw", becomes: "fine"}
.Ed
.Pp
HDR800+, which the camera stores as HDR800 with a priority of 3:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    when: all: [
        {ref: "dynamic_range", equals: "hdr800_plus"},
        {ref: "dynamic_range_priority", equals: "off"},
    ]
    apply: [
        {ref: "dynamic_range", value: "hdr800"},
        {ref: "dynamic_range_priority", value: 0x03},
    ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
A render transformation that writes an inline field:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    when: {ref: "white_balance", equals: "as_shot"}
    apply: [{ref: "white_balance_as_shot", value: 0x01}]
}
.Ed
.Pp
A render rule on the value the image was shot with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    message: "Teleconverter can only be enabled if it was enabled when the image was shot."
    when: all: [
        {ref: "teleconverter", scope: "original", equals: "off"},
        {ref: "teleconverter", equals: "on"},
    ]
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fujicli 1 ,
.Xr fujicli 7
